---
id: org.dxos.review.ui-containers
name: Ui Containers
version: 0.1.0
---

The container/node model for Composer UI. Every collection of objects renders in one of a few
standard **containers**, and every object inside one renders as a **node**:

| Kind      | Primitive                         | Package                                                   |
| --------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| container | list (`Listbox`, `OrderedList`, `Tree`, Mosaic `Stack`) | `@dxos/react-ui-list`, `@dxos/react-ui-mosaic` |
| container | masonry (`Masonry.Root`)          | `@dxos/react-ui-masonry`                                  |
| container | kanban (Mosaic `Board`)           | `@dxos/react-ui-mosaic`                                   |
| container | canvas / spatial board (`Board`)  | `@dxos/react-ui-canvas`, `@dxos/react-ui-board`           |
| node      | card (`Card.*`, `CardTile`)       | `@dxos/react-ui` `Card`, `@dxos/react-ui-card`            |
| node      | article (the object's main view)  | `AppSurface.ObjectArticleProps` surfaces                  |

The containers share one set of hooks (Mosaic `Container`/`Tile`, `Focus.Item`,
`useListNavigation`, `useListSelection`), so navigation, selection, focus, highlighting, scrolling
and reactivity behave the same everywhere. Every card has the same header (icon, title, menu at the
end) and the same three-column layout (start rail, content, end rail), so a user who has learned
one card has learned them all. Content inside a card is not restricted: a card can hold a large text
section. Use these rules unless the user explicitly asks for something else; then say why in a
comment next to the exception.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule collections-use-standard-containers: A collection of objects renders in a standard container — list, masonry, kanban, or canvas
  A plugin that lays out its own tiles (a `grid grid-cols-*` of mapped
  objects, a row of mapped `<section>` columns, a hand-placed flex wrap)
  gets none of the shared navigation, selection, focus, scrolling, or mobile
  behaviour, and looks and acts differently from every other collection in
  the app. Pick the container that fits: list (`Listbox` / `OrderedList` /
  `Tree`, or the Mosaic `Stack` for reorderable surfaces), masonry
  (`Masonry.Root` from `@dxos/react-ui-masonry`), kanban (the Mosaic `Board`
  from `@dxos/react-ui-mosaic`), or canvas (`@dxos/react-ui-canvas`, or the
  spatial `Board` from `@dxos/react-ui-board`). Flag a `map()` over objects
  that produces tiles, cards or columns inside a hand-rolled grid, flex wrap
  or column layout, and a plugin component that re-creates one of these
  containers. `no-hand-rolled-lists` already covers plain rows; this rule
  covers tiles, columns and grids. Do NOT flag a layout the user explicitly
  asked for when a comment says so, or a `map()` producing non-object
  content (chips, grid cells of a table, chart marks).
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: grid-cols|flex-wrap|columns-[0-9]|cols=\{
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule containers-share-core-hooks: A container builds on the shared container hooks, never its own navigation, selection or focus
  The standard containers are interchangeable only because they all route
  keyboard navigation, selection, focus, highlighting and scrolling through
  the same core: Mosaic `Container` / `Tile`, `Focus.Item`,
  `useListNavigation`, `useListSelection`, `useVisibleItems`. A container
  that tracks its own `selectedId` in `useState`, handles arrow keys in its
  own `onKeyDown`, calls `scrollIntoView` itself, or sets `dx-current` /
  `dx-selected` by hand behaves subtly differently from the others and
  breaks when its cards move to another container. New container primitives
  are not wanted: extend an existing container, or the shared hooks, instead.
  Flag a component that renders a collection of tiles and implements any of
  selection state, arrow-key navigation, focus management or scroll-to-item
  itself, and a new container component added outside the container
  packages. Do NOT flag code inside the shared hooks and containers
  themselves (`react-ui-list`, `react-ui-mosaic`, `react-ui-masonry`,
  `react-ui-board`, `react-ui-canvas`, `react-ui` `Focus`), or app-level
  keyboard shortcuts that are not collection navigation.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: ArrowDown|ArrowUp|scrollIntoView|setSelectedId|'dx-current'|'dx-selected'
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule leaf-objects-render-as-cards: Every object inside a container is a card with the standard header
  Users recognise a card by its frame: the icon, the title, and the menu at
  the top right, with content rows below in the three-column layout (start
  rail, content, end rail). A leaf rendered as a bespoke `<section>`, `Panel`,
  or styled `<div>` drops that frame, so each plugin teaches the user a new
  shape for the same idea. Render each leaf with `Card.Root` and a
  `Card.Header` holding the icon, `Card.Title` and `Card.Menu`, and put
  content in `Card.Row` / `Card.Body` / `Card.Section`; inside a Mosaic
  container use `CardTile.Root` + `CardTile.Header` from
  `@dxos/react-ui-card`. Content has no restrictions — a card may hold a
  large text section or a form — only the frame is fixed. Flag a tile or
  item component rendered inside a container that is not built on `Card.*`
  or `CardTile.*`, and a card header assembled from raw elements, or missing
  the title or menu. Do NOT flag the object's main article view, which is a
  node of its own; a section of a document stack (`plugin-stack` sections,
  notebook cells), which shows a whole document and is an article node too;
  or a cell in a table.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: Masonry\.|Mosaic\.|<Board|Listbox\.|OrderedList\.|Stack\b.*Tile|Tile=\{
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule cards-are-container-agnostic: A card renders the same in every container and does not know which one holds it
  A card exists so one object can appear in a list, a masonry, a kanban
  column or on a canvas without being rewritten. A card that reads its
  container's context, takes container-specific props (`column`, `lane`,
  `gridArea`), or exists in several copies per container (`KanbanFooCard`,
  `MasonryFooCard`, `ListFooRow`) breaks that, and the copies drift. Write
  one card per object type, contribute it as the `card--content` surface,
  and let each container supply position, drag handles and selection
  through its own tile wrapper. Flag a card component that imports or reads
  a container's context or props, and two card components for the same
  object type that differ only by the container they are used in. Do NOT
  flag a container-level tile wrapper (Mosaic `Tile`, `Board` item) that
  hosts the shared card.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: Card\.Root|CardTile\.|card--content
  unit: file
  context: imports, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule object-fields-render-as-form: An object's plain properties render as one compact form, not a stack of cards or sections
  Simple properties of one object — an id, a status, some notes — read
  clearest as a standard form: label, value, one row each. Giving each
  property its own card, section or heading spreads three fields over a
  screen, and invents a layout the user has to learn. Render them with the
  schema-driven `Form` from `@dxos/react-ui-form` (inside the card's body or
  the article), or as `Card.Row`s when they are read-only on a card. Flag a
  component that renders one `Card`, `Panel`, `<section>` or heading per
  scalar property of a single object. Do NOT flag properties that are
  themselves objects or collections (those are nodes in a container), or a
  property whose content is large enough to need its own region, such as a
  document body.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: <section|Card\.Section|<h[2-4]\b
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule collection-views-share-one-container: Several views of one collection are tabs of one view container over the same cards
  When a plugin offers the same objects as a board, a kanban and a table,
  each tab written as its own component fetches, sorts and renders the
  objects its own way, so selection and highlighting are lost when the user
  switches, and each view drifts. Use one view container that owns the
  query and the selection, shows sub-tabs for the layouts, and hands the
  same cards to each standard container. Flag sibling tab components that
  each query and render the same collection with their own layout or their
  own item component. Do NOT flag views of different collections, or a
  single view with no alternative layouts.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
  grep: Tabs\.Root|Tabs\.Tab
  unit: file
  context: siblings, imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule container-story-with-fixtures: Every container has a story driven by realistic fixtures and the standard cards
  A container story is how a reviewer sees navigation, selection, scrolling
  and reflow work; a story with three hard-coded `{ id: '1', title: 'a' }`
  items, or one that renders placeholder boxes instead of cards, shows none
  of that, and hides the cases (long titles, many items, empty columns) where
  containers break. Give each container a story with a motivating scenario,
  built from shared fixtures or generators (the package's `testing` entry,
  such as `@dxos/react-ui-mosaic/testing`, or a schema generator) and
  rendering real cards. Flag a container component with no story, and a
  container story whose items are a short inline literal or whose tiles are
  not cards. `story-for-new-ui-component` covers stories in general; this
  rule covers what a container story must contain. Do NOT flag stories of
  non-container components.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/ui/**/*.stories.tsx
    - packages/plugins/**/*.stories.tsx
  grep: Board|Masonry|Stack|Listbox|Kanban|Canvas|Mosaic
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule scroll-list-pads-and-gaps: A scrolling list of cards is padded by its viewport and spaced by its list, so no card touches another or the edge
  Cards stacked flush against each other and against the panel edge read as
  one striped block: the borders merge, the current card's highlight bleeds
  into its neighbours, and the scrollbar overlaps the cards. The spacing has
  one owner per axis: the `ScrollArea.Viewport` pads the content (`p-2`), and
  the list between the cards (`gap-2` on the Mosaic `Stack`, the `gap` of a
  `Layout.Flex` or virtual stack). A card never adds its own outer margin to
  make room. Flag a `ScrollArea.Viewport` with no padding that holds a stack
  or list of cards, a non-draggable Mosaic `Stack` of cards with no gap, and
  a card or tile that sets an outer margin (`m-*`, `my-*`, `mb-*`) to space
  itself from its siblings. Do NOT flag a draggable Mosaic `Stack`, whose
  placeholders already space the tiles; a `Listbox` or `Form`, which own
  their padding (see the composer-ui skill); or rows that are not cards.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: ScrollArea\.Viewport|Mosaic\.Stack|Card\.Root|CardTile\.
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule card-content-in-middle-column: A card whose header uses the rails is a `grid` card, so its rows start in the middle column under the title
  A `Card.Header` that puts an icon in `Layout.Block rail='start'` and a menu
  in `rail='end'` starts its title in the middle column. Without `grid` on
  `Card.Root`, the card is a plain padded column: its `Card.Row`s start at the
  left edge, under the icon instead of under the title, so the body text and
  the title do not line up. `grid` makes the card a rail container and every
  Header, Row and Section a subgrid of it, so all text starts at one content
  edge. Flag a `Card.Root` without `grid` whose `Card.Header` uses
  `rail='start'` or `rail='end'` blocks and which also has `Card.Row` or
  `Card.Section` content. Do NOT flag a card with only a header, or a card
  whose rows set their own `icon` and so already align with the header.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: rail='(start|end)'
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule empty-container-uses-empty: A container with nothing to show renders `Status.Empty`, not a hand-styled message
  Every empty state in the app (no items, no selection, no result, nothing
  opened yet) should look the same: a centred message in the secondary text
  colour, with an optional icon, announced as a status. A hand-rolled
  `<Layout.Flex center classNames='text-description'>` or
  `<div className='p-4 text-description'>` picks its own padding, colour,
  alignment and size, so each panel's empty state looks different, and none
  of them is announced to assistive tech. Render
  `<Status.Empty icon=… classNames='dx-fill'>{t('…empty…')}</Status.Empty>`
  from `@dxos/react-ui/Status` instead (a composite's own `Empty` part is
  built on it). Flag an early return or branch that renders only a
  translated "empty", "no …", "nothing …" or "select/click … to …" message in
  a `Layout.Flex`, `div` or `span` styled by hand. Do NOT flag a `Banner`
  that carries an action, an error message, a loading indicator, or an
  `emptyMessage` prop passed to a component that renders its own empty state.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.empty|empty\.|Empty|no-.*\.(label|message)
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule diagram-links-are-smart: A diagram built in code draws its connectors as `smart` links
  A `line`, `curve` or `spline` link runs straight from port to port, so in
  a laid-out diagram it crosses the cards between its ends and hides their
  text. A `smart` link routes through the lattice gutters (or around the
  boxes when there is no lattice) and picks the ports that bend least, so
  arrows never cross a card. Flag a `link: { type: 'line' | 'curve' |
  'spline' }` passed to `createDynamicProjection` or to a dynamic layout's
  options, and a link created by code for a generated diagram with one of
  those types. Do NOT flag a link a user drew on a free canvas, the link-type
  picker itself, or tests of the other link types.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/*.ts
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/ui/**/*.ts
    - packages/ui/**/*.tsx
  grep: type:\s*'(line|curve|spline)'
  unit: file
  context: imports
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent or the System One checker.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:       Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:       Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:   warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:      dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
    unit?:       file | pr         # one verdict per file (default) or per change set
    context?:    ContextKind[]     # diff | imports | importers | siblings | package | public-api | similar | test | pr
    system-one?: on | off          # off when no fetcher can supply what the rule needs
    question?:   Prose             # literal yes/no question for the System One checker
```
